PATTERN CATALOGUE

The taxonomy follows publicly documented signs of AI writing. The vocabulary
lists in references/patterns.md sections 1, 9 and 46 are drawn from Pangram
Labs' published measurements of words over-represented in machine text, and
each use cites that source in place; the word lists compiled into
scripts/detect.py draw on the same measurements. Facts about how machines
write are not anyone's property. Every worked example, every Before and After
pair and every line of detector code here was written for this project, with
one exception: the h_classic_prose sample in tests/validation_corpus.py opens
with a sentence from Dickens, "A Tale of Two Cities" (1859), long in the
public domain.

BENCHMARK MATERIAL

No third-party corpus is distributed in this repository. The public matched
measurements cited in README.md and CITATION.cff use the MIT-licensed Human
Detectors repository release at a pinned revision; the source, revision, file hash,
protocol and results are recorded in docs/research/. Users obtain upstream data
under its own terms. A separate current-model arm was generated for this audit
and is identified as self-generated rather than an independent public dataset.

The original release also cited private benchmark figures. They are no longer
used to support the current product headline.

STATEMENT OF PURPOSE AND NON-GOALS

sloptrim improves the quality of prose. It is not a tool for concealing the
origin of a document, and it is not built or maintained to defeat any
detector.

It does optimise against its own published quality score, which is what an
editing tool does. It consults no third-party detector of any kind. See
ETHICS.md for why that distinction is the whole position.

The project will not accept, and the maintainer will not implement:

  - any check against a third-party AI detector, hosted or local
  - any objective tied to a third-party detector's score
  - any feature whose stated purpose is to pass a plagiarism, integrity or
    authorship check
  - any claim that using this tool makes undisclosed AI assistance
    acceptable, permitted or undetectable

Better writing is harder to distinguish from human writing, because
detectors key on bad writing. That is a property of detectors, not a
feature of this tool, and it is not a purpose it is optimised for.

If you did not write it, sloptrim does not make it yours.

Three requests, which are requests and not licence conditions. Apache-2.0
permits what it permits:

  - Do not use this to conceal AI assistance where disclosure is required
    of you.
  - Do not advertise it as a way to defeat a detector. It is not built for
    that and no such claim is made anywhere in it.
  - If you publish numbers about it, say what you measured them on. No corpus
    is bundled here; record the upstream revision, split and protocol.

See ETHICS.md for the full position, including academic use.


